<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>With-Gpt</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/categories/with-gpt/</link><description>Technical notes by Jaehun Ryu, a deep learning compiler engineer, on LLM inference, KV caches, compilers, C++, AI papers, and open-source code.</description><language>en-US</language><atom:link href="https://jaehun.me/en/categories/with-gpt/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>[Paper Review] Conditional Memory via Scalable Lookup: A New Axis of Sparsity for Large Language Models</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-conditional-memory-via-scalable-lookup-a-new-axis-of-sparsity-for-large-language-models/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-conditional-memory-via-scalable-lookup-a-new-axis-of-sparsity-for-large-language-models/</guid><pubDate>Wed, 14 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.07372v1"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="engram-the-second-sparsity-axis-after-conditional-computation-moe-conditional-memory"&gt;Engram: The Second Sparsity Axis After Conditional Computation (MoE), Conditional Memory&lt;a href="#engram-the-second-sparsity-axis-after-conditional-computation-moe-conditional-memory" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h2 id="one-line-summary-tldr"&gt;One-Line Summary (TL;DR)&lt;a href="#one-line-summary-tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Engram adds a &amp;ldquo;knowledge lookup primitive&amp;rdquo; to the Transformer, offloading the static-knowledge/local-pattern reconstruction burden performed by standard models in early layers to &lt;strong&gt;constant-time $O(1)$ N-gram memory lookup&lt;/strong&gt;, and simultaneously shows broad benchmark gains over MoE under iso-activated conditions — &lt;strong&gt;MMLU +3.0, BBH +5.0&lt;/strong&gt; — together with a sharp &lt;strong&gt;NIAH-MQ +12.8pt (iso-loss)&lt;/strong&gt; jump in 32k long context.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>[Paper Review] Attention Sinks and Compression Valleys in LLMs are Two Sides of the Same Coin</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-attention-sinks-and-compression-valleys-in-llms-are-two-sides-of-the-same-coin/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-attention-sinks-and-compression-valleys-in-llms-are-two-sides-of-the-same-coin/</guid><pubDate>Thu, 23 Oct 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06477v1"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="one-narrative-forged-by-massive-activations-bridging-attention-sink-and-compression-valley"&gt;One Narrative Forged by Massive Activations: Bridging &lt;strong&gt;Attention Sink&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Compression Valley&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;a href="#one-narrative-forged-by-massive-activations-bridging-attention-sink-and-compression-valley" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h2 id="tldr"&gt;TL;DR&lt;a href="#tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Massive activations in the residual stream (especially BOS)&lt;/strong&gt; form a &lt;strong&gt;dominant singular value&lt;/strong&gt;, which simultaneously induces &lt;strong&gt;mid-layer representation compression (entropy↓)&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;attention sink (attention drawn to BOS)&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;in the same layer&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;targeted MLP ablation&lt;/strong&gt; provides &lt;strong&gt;causal confirmation&lt;/strong&gt; of this. The &lt;strong&gt;optimal depth&lt;/strong&gt; for generation, reasoning, and embedding is explained within a single &lt;strong&gt;Mix–Compress–Refine&lt;/strong&gt; framework (source: §Abstract, §3.1–§3.3, Fig.1–4, §4–§5).&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>[Paper Review] Hardware-Efficient Attention for Fast Decoding</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-hardware-efficient-attention-for-fast-decoding/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-hardware-efficient-attention-for-fast-decoding/</guid><pubDate>Thu, 23 Oct 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.21487v1"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="gta--gla-hardware-efficient-attention-that-breaks-the-memory-dominated-decode"&gt;GTA &amp;amp; GLA: Hardware-Efficient Attention That Breaks the &amp;lsquo;Memory-Dominated&amp;rsquo; Decode&lt;a href="#gta--gla-hardware-efficient-attention-that-breaks-the-memory-dominated-decode" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h2 id="tldr"&gt;TL;DR&lt;a href="#tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GTA (key–value tying) and GLA (latent head sharding)&lt;/strong&gt; raise &lt;strong&gt;FLOPs/byte&lt;/strong&gt; and shrink the &lt;strong&gt;per-GPU KV cache&lt;/strong&gt;, achieving &lt;strong&gt;1.2–2× kernel speedups&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;sharply lower serving latency&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;up to 2× higher throughput&lt;/strong&gt; — &lt;strong&gt;without quality loss&lt;/strong&gt; (source: §Abstract, Fig.4–5, Fig.15, Tab.38–41).&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>[Paper Review] Towards Efficient and Practical GPU Multitasking in the Era of LLM</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-towards-efficient-and-practical-gpu-multitasking-in-the-era-of-llm/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-towards-efficient-and-practical-gpu-multitasking-in-the-era-of-llm/</guid><pubDate>Thu, 09 Oct 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.08448v1"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="lets-put-an-os-on-the-gpu-a-proposal-for-a-gpu-multitasking-os-layer-for-the-llm-era"&gt;&amp;ldquo;Let&amp;rsquo;s put an OS on the GPU&amp;rdquo;: A proposal for a &lt;strong&gt;GPU multitasking OS layer&lt;/strong&gt; for the LLM era&lt;a href="#lets-put-an-os-on-the-gpu-a-proposal-for-a-gpu-multitasking-os-layer-for-the-llm-era" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h2 id="one-line-summary-tldr"&gt;One-line summary (TL;DR)&lt;a href="#one-line-summary-tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;In LLM serving, GPUs often stay at &lt;strong&gt;≤10% utilization&lt;/strong&gt; (field observation), and load fluctuates by &lt;strong&gt;3× within minutes&lt;/strong&gt;. The authors propose a &lt;strong&gt;&amp;ldquo;GPU multitasking OS layer&amp;rdquo;&lt;/strong&gt; that combines &lt;strong&gt;kernel-grain time/space sharing + memory multitasking based on CUDA virtual memory (2 MB) + Guaranteed/Preemptible resource coordination&lt;/strong&gt;, and argue that this is the only realistic solution to simultaneously achieve &lt;strong&gt;utilization, performance guarantees, isolation, and large-scale deployment&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>[Paper Review] Marconi: Prefix Caching for the Era of Hybrid LLMs</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-marconi-prefix-caching-for-the-era-of-hybrid-llms/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-marconi-prefix-caching-for-the-era-of-hybrid-llms/</guid><pubDate>Wed, 08 Oct 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.19379v3"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper Link&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="marconi-rethinking-prefix-caching-for-the-hybrid-llm-era"&gt;Marconi: Rethinking Prefix Caching for the Hybrid LLM Era&lt;a href="#marconi-rethinking-prefix-caching-for-the-hybrid-llm-era" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h2 id="tldr"&gt;TL;DR&lt;a href="#tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Marconi&lt;/strong&gt; introduces a prefix-caching framework for hybrid LLM inference (Attention + SSM).&lt;br&gt;&#10;It selectively &lt;strong&gt;admits only highly-reusable prefixes&lt;/strong&gt; and applies &lt;strong&gt;FLOP-per-byte–aware eviction&lt;/strong&gt;, yielding up to &lt;strong&gt;34.4× higher token-hit rate&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;71.1 % lower P95 TTFT (−617 ms)&lt;/strong&gt; compared with enhanced baselines (vLLM+, SGLang+).&lt;br&gt;&#10;(Evidence: Abstract, Fig. 7–9, § 5.2)&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>[Paper Review] SGLang: Efficient Execution of Structured Language Model Programs</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-sglang-efficient-execution-of-structured-language-model-programs/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-sglang-efficient-execution-of-structured-language-model-programs/</guid><pubDate>Fri, 03 Oct 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.07104v2"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper Link&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="sglang--radixattention-how-execution-optimization-for-lm-programs-achieved-a-64x-speedup"&gt;SGLang &amp;amp; RadixAttention: How Execution Optimization for &amp;ldquo;LM Programs&amp;rdquo; Achieved a 6.4x Speedup&lt;a href="#sglang--radixattention-how-execution-optimization-for-lm-programs-achieved-a-64x-speedup" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h2 id="tldr"&gt;TL;DR&lt;a href="#tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;By combining a language-runtime &lt;strong&gt;co-design&lt;/strong&gt; with &lt;strong&gt;RadixAttention (radix tree-based KV cache + scheduling)&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;Compressed FSM (multi-token constrained decoding)&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;API speculative execution&lt;/strong&gt;, this work achieves up to &lt;strong&gt;6.4x higher throughput&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;3.7x lower latency&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;1.7x lower average TTFT&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;1.6x faster JSON/Regex decoding&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;~6x faster multimodal performance&lt;/strong&gt; across various LM programs. Even with no cache reuse opportunities, the &lt;strong&gt;overhead is &amp;lt;0.3%&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>[Paper Review] Transformers are SSMs: Generalized Models and Efficient Algorithms Through Structured State Space Duality</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-transformers-are-ssms-generalized-models-and-efficient-algorithms-through-structured-state-space-duality/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-transformers-are-ssms-generalized-models-and-efficient-algorithms-through-structured-state-space-duality/</guid><pubDate>Fri, 03 Oct 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.21060v1"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper Link&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="structured-state-space-duality-unifying-ssms-and-attention-with-mamba-2-for-28-acceleration"&gt;Structured State Space Duality: Unifying SSMs and Attention with Mamba-2 for 2–8× Acceleration&lt;a href="#structured-state-space-duality-unifying-ssms-and-attention-with-mamba-2-for-28-acceleration" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h3 id="tldr"&gt;TL;DR&lt;a href="#tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Structured State-Space Duality (SSD)&lt;/strong&gt; reveals that SSMs and masked attention compute the &lt;strong&gt;same function&lt;/strong&gt; with different &lt;strong&gt;contraction orders&lt;/strong&gt;. It introduces a &lt;strong&gt;matmul-dominant kernel&lt;/strong&gt; based on &lt;strong&gt;block decomposition and low-rank&lt;/strong&gt; properties, achieving &lt;strong&gt;2–8× speedup&lt;/strong&gt; over Mamba-scan and outperforming FlashAttention-2 for sequences &lt;strong&gt;longer than 2k tokens&lt;/strong&gt;. In a &lt;strong&gt;2.7B / 300B tokens&lt;/strong&gt; pre-training setup, it matches the quality of &lt;strong&gt;Transformer++&lt;/strong&gt; and shows further improvement to a &lt;strong&gt;PPL of 5.95&lt;/strong&gt; when &lt;strong&gt;mixed with 6 attention layers&lt;/strong&gt; (Source: Abstract, Fig. 10, Tab. 3, §9.2–9.3).&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>[Paper Review] Inference-Time Hyper-Scaling with KV Cache Compression</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-inference-time-hyper-scaling-with-kv-cache-compression/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-inference-time-hyper-scaling-with-kv-cache-compression/</guid><pubDate>Tue, 29 Jul 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.05345v1"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Link to Paper&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="dynamic-memory-sparsification-dms-making-llm-hyper-scaling-a-reality-with-8-kv-cache-compression"&gt;Dynamic Memory Sparsification (DMS): Making LLM Hyper-Scaling a Reality with 8× KV Cache Compression&lt;a href="#dynamic-memory-sparsification-dms-making-llm-hyper-scaling-a-reality-with-8-kv-cache-compression" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h2 id="one-line-summary-tldr"&gt;One-Line Summary (TL;DR)&lt;a href="#one-line-summary-tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;DMS&lt;/strong&gt;, combining a lightweight re-fitting of just &lt;code&gt;1K&lt;/code&gt; steps with a &lt;strong&gt;delayed eviction strategy&lt;/strong&gt;, achieves up to &lt;strong&gt;8×&lt;/strong&gt; KV cache compression while paradoxically boosting performance, such as a &lt;strong&gt;+9.1 pt&lt;/strong&gt; gain on AIME 24 for Qwen-R1 32B. This allows for generating &lt;strong&gt;longer and more&lt;/strong&gt; tokens in real-time within the same compute and memory budget.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>[Paper Review] Llama-Nemotron: Efficient Reasoning Models</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-llama-nemotron-efficient-reasoning-models/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-llama-nemotron-efficient-reasoning-models/</guid><pubDate>Tue, 29 Jul 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.00949v4"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper Link&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="hydragen-the-secret-weapon-for-decoding-large-batches-with-shared-prefixes-up-to-32-faster"&gt;Hydragen: The Secret Weapon for Decoding Large Batches with Shared Prefixes up to 32× Faster&lt;a href="#hydragen-the-secret-weapon-for-decoding-large-batches-with-shared-prefixes-up-to-32-faster" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h2 id="tldr"&gt;TL;DR&lt;a href="#tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;By decomposing the prefix and suffix using &lt;em&gt;softmax denominator rescaling&lt;/em&gt;, Hydragen accelerates long-context, large-batch decoding on &lt;strong&gt;Code-Llama-13B&lt;/strong&gt; by up to &lt;em&gt;32x&lt;/em&gt; compared to vLLM, all while guaranteeing bit-for-bit identical output.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>[Paper Review] KIMI K2: OPEN AGENTIC INTELLIGENCE</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-kimi-k2-open-agentic-intelligence/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-kimi-k2-open-agentic-intelligence/</guid><pubDate>Sat, 26 Jul 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://github.com/MoonshotAI/Kimi-K2/blob/main/tech_report.pdf"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper Link&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="kimik2-an-open-source-llms-leap-toward-agentic-intelligence"&gt;Kimi K2: An Open-Source LLM’s Leap Toward Agentic Intelligence&lt;a href="#kimik2-an-open-source-llms-leap-toward-agentic-intelligence" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h2 id="tldr"&gt;TL;DR&lt;a href="#tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;With a 3-stage pipeline consisting of &lt;em&gt;MuonClip pretraining + large-scale agentic tool-use data + Verifiable RL alignment&lt;/em&gt;, Kimi K2 achieves 66.1 on τ²-Bench and 65.8 on SWE-bench—outperforming previous open-source models by over 10 points and approaching GPT-4-level long-term reasoning and tool-use performance.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>[Paper Review] Qwen 3 Technical Report</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-qwen-3-technical-report/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-qwen-3-technical-report/</guid><pubDate>Sat, 26 Jul 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.09388v1"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper Link&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="qwen-3-the-evolution-of-a-giant-moe-language-model-with-adjustable-reasoning-depth"&gt;Qwen 3: The Evolution of a Giant MoE Language Model with Adjustable Reasoning Depth&lt;a href="#qwen-3-the-evolution-of-a-giant-moe-language-model-with-adjustable-reasoning-depth" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h2 id="tldr-in-one-line"&gt;TL;DR (in one line)&lt;a href="#tldr-in-one-line" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Qwen 3&lt;/strong&gt; couples a user-controllable &lt;em&gt;Thinking Budget&lt;/em&gt; with a 128-expert MoE backbone, achieving closed-source–level performance and multilingual breadth while activating only &lt;strong&gt;22 B&lt;/strong&gt; parameters out of a &lt;strong&gt;235 B&lt;/strong&gt; total.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>[Paper Review] Massive Activations in Large Language Models</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-massive-activations-in-large-language-models/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-massive-activations-in-large-language-models/</guid><pubDate>Wed, 09 Jul 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17762v2"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper Link&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="massive-activations-hidden-biases-a-reinterpretation-of-self-attentions-secrets"&gt;Massive Activations, Hidden Biases: A Reinterpretation of Self-Attention’s Secrets&lt;a href="#massive-activations-hidden-biases-a-reinterpretation-of-self-attentions-secrets" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;hr&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="tldr"&gt;TL;DR&lt;a href="#tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Just &lt;strong&gt;4–10 extreme scalar values (×10,000)&lt;/strong&gt; out of tens of millions can single-handedly uphold the performance of LLMs and ViTs.&lt;br&gt;&#10;These scalars act as &lt;strong&gt;input-invariant, constant self-attention biases&lt;/strong&gt;—zeroing them causes immediate model collapse (PPL → ∞).&lt;br&gt;&#10;The authors replace these implicit phenomena with an &lt;strong&gt;Explicit Attention Bias (EAB)&lt;/strong&gt; module, fully &lt;strong&gt;restoring performance&lt;/strong&gt; while enabling better analysis, compression, and safety.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>[Paper Review] Peri-LN: Revisiting Normalization Layer in the Transformer Architecture</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-peri-ln-revisiting-normalization-layer-in-the-transformer-architecture/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-peri-ln-revisiting-normalization-layer-in-the-transformer-architecture/</guid><pubDate>Wed, 09 Jul 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.02732v3"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper Link&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="peri-layernorm-a-third-option-beyond-post-ln-and-pre-ln"&gt;Peri-LayerNorm: A Third Option Beyond Post-LN and Pre-LN&lt;a href="#peri-layernorm-a-third-option-beyond-post-ln-and-pre-ln" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h2 id="tldr"&gt;TL;DR&lt;a href="#tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;By simply adding another LayerNorm right after the residual connection—&amp;ldquo;Peri-LayerNorm (Peri-LN)&amp;quot;—the authors eliminate FP16 overflows entirely (from 400M to 3.2B LLMs) and improve average accuracy on five benchmarks by up to +2.8 pp.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>[paper review] SageAttention3: Microscaling FP4 Attention for Inference and An Exploration of 8-bit Training</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-sageattention3-microscaling-fp4-attention-for-inference-and-an-exploration-of-8-bit-training/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-sageattention3-microscaling-fp4-attention-for-inference-and-an-exploration-of-8-bit-training/</guid><pubDate>Wed, 09 Jul 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.11594v1"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper v1&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt; · &lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/html/2505.11594v1"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Full text&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="separate-inference-from-training"&gt;Separate inference from training&lt;a href="#separate-inference-from-training" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;SageAttention3 targets &lt;strong&gt;FP4 inference&lt;/strong&gt;; SageBwd targets &lt;strong&gt;INT8 training&lt;/strong&gt;. Of seven forward/backward matmuls, $dOV^\top$ stays FP16. (§3–4)&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>[Paper Review] Helix Parallelism: Rethinking Sharding Strategies for Interactive Multi-Million-Token LLM Decoding</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-helix-parallelism-rethinking-sharding-strategies-for-interactive-multi-million-token-llm-decoding/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/paper-review-helix-parallelism-rethinking-sharding-strategies-for-interactive-multi-million-token-llm-decoding/</guid><pubDate>Tue, 08 Jul 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://research.nvidia.com/publication/2025-07_helix-parallelism-rethinking-sharding-strategies-interactive-multi-million"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper Link&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="helix-parallelism-breaking-the-latency-throughput-wall-of-ultra-long-llm-decoding"&gt;Helix Parallelism: Breaking the Latency-Throughput Wall of Ultra-Long LLM Decoding&lt;a href="#helix-parallelism-breaking-the-latency-throughput-wall-of-ultra-long-llm-decoding" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h2 id="tldr"&gt;TL;DR&lt;a href="#tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Helix Parallelism schedules Attention and FFN with different parallelism strategies to eliminate KV cache duplication and FFN weight load bottlenecks—reducing token latency by up to 1.5× for 1M-token contexts and increasing concurrent user capacity by up to 32× under the same latency budget.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>DeepSeek-Prover-V2: Advancing Formal Mathematical Reasoning via Reinforcement Learning for Subgoal Decomposition</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/deepseek-prover-v2-advancing-formal-mathematical-reasoning-via-reinforcement-learning-for-subgoal-decomposition/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/deepseek-prover-v2-advancing-formal-mathematical-reasoning-via-reinforcement-learning-for-subgoal-decomposition/</guid><pubDate>Tue, 08 Jul 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.21801v1"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="subgoal-curriculum--cot-consistency-deepseek-prover-v2-reshapes-automated-theorem-proving"&gt;Subgoal Curriculum + CoT Consistency: &lt;strong&gt;DeepSeek-Prover-V2&lt;/strong&gt; Reshapes Automated Theorem Proving&lt;a href="#subgoal-curriculum--cot-consistency-deepseek-prover-v2-reshapes-automated-theorem-proving" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h2 id="tldr"&gt;TL;DR&lt;a href="#tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;DeepSeek-Prover-V2&lt;/em&gt;, built on the principle of &lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;breaking a problem into small pieces and then matching them all the way through,&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt; achieves a new SOTA on MiniF2F Pass@32 of &lt;strong&gt;82.4%&lt;/strong&gt; (671B) and &lt;strong&gt;75.6%&lt;/strong&gt; (7B) even in a small 7B model. The key is the combination of &lt;strong&gt;Subgoal-guided Curriculum&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Chain-of-Thought-Lean consistency reward (GRPO)&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Inference-Time Scaling for Generalist Reward Modeling</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/inference-time-scaling-for-generalist-reward-modeling/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/inference-time-scaling-for-generalist-reward-modeling/</guid><pubDate>Tue, 08 Jul 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.02495v2"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="inference-time-scaling-how-deepseek-grm-surpassed-giant-models"&gt;Inference-Time Scaling: How DeepSeek-GRM Surpassed Giant Models&lt;a href="#inference-time-scaling-how-deepseek-grm-surpassed-giant-models" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h2 id="one-line-summary-tldr"&gt;One-Line Summary (TL;DR)&lt;a href="#one-line-summary-tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&amp;ldquo;27B model × 32 samples&amp;rdquo;&lt;/strong&gt;—With only a Generative Reward Model (GRM) and k-Vote summation, it records &lt;strong&gt;72.8% overall accuracy&lt;/strong&gt;, higher than GPT-4o and Nemotron-340B, presenting &lt;em&gt;inference compute instead of model size&lt;/em&gt; as a new scaling axis.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Code I/O: Condensing Reasoning Patterns via Code Input-Output Prediction</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/code-i/o-condensing-reasoning-patterns-via-code-input-output-prediction/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/code-i/o-condensing-reasoning-patterns-via-code-input-output-prediction/</guid><pubDate>Mon, 07 Jul 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.07316v4"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="code-io-from-code-io--natural-language-cot-to-general-purpose-reasoning--lifting-7b-30b-llms-by-2-points-on-average-with-data-design-alone"&gt;CODE I/O: From Code I/O + Natural-Language CoT to General-Purpose Reasoning — Lifting 7B-30B LLMs by +2 Points on Average with Data Design Alone&lt;a href="#code-io-from-code-io--natural-language-cot-to-general-purpose-reasoning--lifting-7b-30b-llms-by-2-points-on-average-with-data-design-alone" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h2 id="tldr"&gt;TL;DR&lt;a href="#tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A single data pipeline of &amp;ldquo;code function → input/output prediction + structured Chain-of-Thought (CoT)&amp;rdquo; lets 3.5 M samples deliver larger and more balanced gains (+2.9 points) than 14 M-scale SOTA data.&lt;/strong&gt;&#10;By nailing verifiability, low cost, and diversity at once, &lt;strong&gt;CODE I/O&lt;/strong&gt; empirically demonstrates that &amp;ldquo;data quality &amp;gt; data quantity.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Native Sparse Attention: Hardware-Aligned and Natively Trainable Sparse Attention</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/native-sparse-attention-hardware-aligned-and-natively-trainable-sparse-attention/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/native-sparse-attention-hardware-aligned-and-natively-trainable-sparse-attention/</guid><pubDate>Mon, 07 Jul 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.11089v2"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="native-sparse-attention-nsa--11-faster-even-at-64k-tokens-accuracy-intact"&gt;Native Sparse Attention (NSA) — 11× faster even at 64k tokens, accuracy intact&lt;a href="#native-sparse-attention-nsa--11-faster-even-at-64k-tokens-accuracy-intact" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h2 id="one-line-summary-tldr"&gt;One-line summary (TL;DR)&lt;a href="#one-line-summary-tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;NSA combines a three-branch sparse attention — “compress → select → slide” — with a GQA/MQA-friendly kernel to make decoding 11.6× faster and training up to 9× faster at 64k context, while improving average performance over Full Attention.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Janus-Pro: Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation with Data and Model Scaling</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/janus-pro-unifiedmultimodalunderstanding-and-generation-with-data-and-model-scaling/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/janus-pro-unifiedmultimodalunderstanding-and-generation-with-data-and-model-scaling/</guid><pubDate>Sun, 06 Jul 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.17811v1"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="janus-pro-7b-dual-encoder-multimodal-llm-that-outsmarts-bigger-models"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Janus-Pro 7B: Dual-Encoder Multimodal LLM That Outsmarts Bigger Models&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;a href="#janus-pro-7b-dual-encoder-multimodal-llm-that-outsmarts-bigger-models" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;h2 id="one-line-summary-tldr"&gt;One-line summary (TL;DR)&lt;a href="#one-line-summary-tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;By &lt;em&gt;fully separating the SigLIP understanding encoder from the VQ generation encoder&lt;/em&gt; and attaching them to a &lt;strong&gt;7 B-parameter LLM&lt;/strong&gt; with a &amp;lsquo;Dual-Encoder + Adapter&amp;rsquo; design, it becomes the first case to &lt;strong&gt;beat a 13 B unified model (TokenFlow-XL) on both understanding and generation at the same time&lt;/strong&gt;. – &lt;strong&gt;MMBench 79.2 (+10.3 pt) / GenEval 0.80 (+45 %)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>DeepSeek-V3 Technical Report</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/deepseek-v3-technical-report/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/deepseek-v3-technical-report/</guid><pubDate>Sat, 05 Jul 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.19437v2"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="one-line-summary-tldr"&gt;One-line Summary (TL;DR)&lt;a href="#one-line-summary-tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;DeepSeek-V3 is an open-source SOTA that combines &lt;code&gt;Aux-loss-free Load-Balancing Bias&lt;/code&gt;, FP8 mixed-precision training, and Multi-Token Prediction in a 671B-parameter MoE LLM to reach parity with (or surpass) a dense 405B model at less than half the GPU time and cost.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>JanusFlow: Harmonizing Autoregression and Rectified Flow for Unified Multimodal Understanding and Generation</title><link>https://jaehun.me/en/posts/janusflow-harmonizing-autoregression-and-rectified-flow-for-unified-multimodal-understanding-and-generation/</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://jaehun.me/en/posts/janusflow-harmonizing-autoregression-and-rectified-flow-for-unified-multimodal-understanding-and-generation/</guid><pubDate>Wed, 02 Jul 2025 00:00:00 +0900</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a&#10; href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.07975v2"target="_blank"&#10; class="inline-flex items-center gap-1"&#10; &gt;Paper&lt;svg class="h-3 w-3 flex-shrink-0" id="external-link" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24"&gt;&lt;path fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M15 3h6v6m-11 5L21 3m-3 10v6a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V8a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h6"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="one-line-summary-tldr"&gt;One-line summary (TL;DR)&lt;a href="#one-line-summary-tldr" class="heading-anchor" aria-label="Link to this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;JanusFlow&lt;/strong&gt; achieves &lt;strong&gt;state-of-the-art performance in both image understanding and generation&lt;/strong&gt; (FID 9.51 / GenEval 0.63 / MMBench 74.9) with a single 1.3 B-parameter model built on &lt;em&gt;Rectified Flow&lt;/em&gt; + &lt;em&gt;decoupled vision encoders&lt;/em&gt; + &lt;em&gt;representation alignment&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>